  <h2>What Does Beef Jerky Seasoning Actually Do?</h2>
  <p>Seasoning serves three functions in beef jerky: <strong>preservation</strong> (salt draws moisture out and inhibits bacterial growth), <strong>flavor</strong> (spices, aromatics, umami compounds), and <strong>texture</strong> (salt denatures surface proteins, affecting how the finished jerky chews). Understanding these roles helps you build better blends and troubleshoot problems.</p>

  <h2>The Best Beef Jerky Seasoning Recipe (Core Formula)</h2>
  <p>This base works for any flavor direction:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salt:</strong> 1–1.5 tsp per pound of raw beef</li>
    <li><strong>Black pepper:</strong> 1/2–1 tsp per pound</li>
    <li><strong>Garlic powder:</strong> 1/2 tsp per pound</li>
    <li><strong>Onion powder:</strong> 1/2 tsp per pound</li>
    <li><strong>Liquid base:</strong> 3 tbsp soy sauce + 1 tbsp Worcestershire per pound</li>
  </ul>
  <p>From this base, you branch into flavor profiles by adding:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spicy:</strong> cayenne, red pepper flakes, sriracha, gochujang</li>
    <li><strong>Sweet:</strong> brown sugar, honey, maple syrup</li>
    <li><strong>Smoky:</strong> smoked paprika, liquid smoke, chipotle powder</li>
    <li><strong>Teriyaki:</strong> honey, rice vinegar, fresh ginger</li>
    <li><strong>BBQ:</strong> tomato paste, apple cider vinegar, liquid smoke — see the full <a href="/recipes/bbq-beef-jerky/">honey BBQ jerky recipe</a></li>
  </ul>

